    <!-- 1. INTRODUCTION -->
    <section class="chapter" id="intro">
      <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">01 — Introduction</span>What is OS Security?</h2>
      <p>Every time you use your computer, the <strong>Operating System (OS)</strong> is working behind the scenes. It's
        like the security guard of a large building. It decides who is allowed to enter, which rooms they can visit, and
        what they can touch.</p>
      <p>Think of the OS like a hotel. The <strong>Kernel</strong> is the hotel manager who holds all the master keys.
        The <strong>Processes</strong> (apps like Chrome or Word) are the guests. <strong>Files</strong> are the rooms.
        The <strong>Security Model</strong> is the list of rules that says which guest can enter which room.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>The Golden Rule</strong>
        Both Windows and Linux follow one main rule: <em>Don't give everyone all the power.</em> Most of the time, you
        only have the power you need to do your job. This keeps the whole system safe if one part gets into trouble.
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub">The Three Goals of Security (CIA)</h3>
      <p>To keep a computer safe, security experts focus on three things, called the <strong>CIA Triad</strong>:</p>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🔒</div>
          <div class="card-title">Confidentiality (Privacy)</div>
          <p>Only people with permission can see your data. Like a locked diary that only you have the key for.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">✏️</div>
          <div class="card-title">Integrity (Trust)</div>
          <p>Making sure data isn't changed by mistake or by a hacker. Like making sure no one changes the numbers on
            your bank statement.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">⚡</div>
          <div class="card-title">Availability (Access)</div>
          <p>Making sure you can actually use your computer when you need to. Like making sure the hotel door isn't
            glued shut.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

