    <!-- 2. HISTORY -->
    <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">02 — History</span>Why Security Matters Now</h2>
    <p>In the early days, computers weren't connected to the internet, so security wasn't a priority. As we started
      connecting everything, hackers found ways to break in, forcing Windows and Linux to build better "locks."</p>

    <h3 class="sub">The Evolution of Locks</h3>
    <p>Think of it like this:</p>
    <ul class="body-list">
      <li><strong>Phase 1: No Locks.</strong> Early computers had no security. Anyone could do anything.</li>
      <li><strong>Phase 2: Simple Padlocks.</strong> Computers started using passwords, but they were easy to bypass.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Phase 3: Deadbolts &amp; Alarms.</strong> Modern systems have many layers of protection, like biometrics
        (fingerprints) and encryption (scrambling data).</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout warn">
      <strong>The Big Lesson</strong>
      Security is a constant race. As hackers get smarter, the "locks" on our computers have to get stronger.
    </div>
    

    <!-- 3. ARCHITECTURE -->
    <section class="chapter" id="architecture">
      <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">03 — Architecture</span>How Computers Protect Their "Brain"</h2>
      <p>The **Kernel** is the most important part of the OS—it's the brain. To keep it safe, computers use a system of
        **Rings** (Privilege Levels).</p>
      <p>Imagine a castle. The King (the Kernel) lives in the innermost room (Ring 0). The regular people (your apps)
        live in the outer courtyard (Ring 3). People in the courtyard can't just walk into the King's room without
        permission. This way, if something goes wrong in the courtyard, the King stays safe.</p>

      <h3 class="sub">Windows vs. Linux: A Simple Look</h3>
