    <!-- 4. USER ACCOUNTS -->
    <section class="chapter" id="users">
      <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">04 — Users</span>Who Are You on This Computer?</h2>
      <p>Computers need to know who is using them so they can decide what that person is allowed to do. This is called
        the <strong>User Model</strong>.</p>

      <h3 class="sub">Windows Accounts</h3>
      <ul class="body-list">
        <li><strong>Standard User:</strong> Good for everyday stuff. You can't change major settings or break the
          system. This is the safest way to use your computer.</li>
        <li><strong>Administrator:</strong> The boss. Can install software and change any setting.</li>
        <li><strong>SYSTEM:</strong> The "God Mode" of the computer. It's the account the computer uses to manage
          itself. You can't log into it directly.</li>
      </ul>

      <h4 class="subsub">User Account Control (UAC)</h4>
      <p>You know that box that pops up and asks, "Do you want to allow this app to make changes?" That's **UAC**. It's
        like a security guard asking for your ID before letting you do something important.</p>

      <h3 class="sub">Linux Accounts</h3>
      <ul class="body-list">
        <li><strong>Regular User:</strong> Similar to a Standard User in Windows.</li>
        <li><strong>root:</strong> The ultimate boss. Has total power over the entire system. In Linux, we say "root is
          god."</li>
        <li><strong>sudo:</strong> A command that lets a regular user act like the boss (root) for just one task.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout success">
        <strong>Real-World Analogy</strong>
        Think of your computer like an airport. A regular user is like a traveler—they can only go to their gate.
        **root** or **SYSTEM** is like the airport manager—they have a badge that opens every single door.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 5. FILESYSTEM -->
    <section class="chapter" id="filesystem">
      <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">05 — Filesystem</span>Who Can Touch My Files?</h2>
      <p>The OS uses "Permissions" to decide who can read, change, or run a file.</p>

      <h3 class="sub">Linux: The rwx System</h3>
      <p>Linux uses three simple letters to control files:</p>
      <ul class="body-list">
        <li><strong>r (Read):</strong> Can you see what's inside?</li>
        <li><strong>w (Write):</strong> Can you change it or delete it?</li>
        <li><strong>x (Execute):</strong> Can you run it as a program?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These rules are set for the **Owner**, the **Group**, and **Others** (everyone else).</p>

      <div class="callout warn">
        <strong>Analogy</strong>
        Imagine a communal fridge. You can **Read** (look at the food), **Write** (eat or change the food), and
        **Execute** (actually cook the food). Some food is only for you (Owner), some for your roommates (Group), and
        some for guests (Others).
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub">Windows: Permission Folders</h3>
      <p>Windows is very detailed. You can right-click any folder, go to "Security," and see exactly who can do what.
        You can give someone permission to **Read** a file but not **Delete** it. </p>

      <div class="callout danger">
        <strong>Common Mistake</strong>
        In Windows, if you give someone permission to a folder, they usually get permission to everything *inside* that
        folder too. Always be careful when sharing big folders!
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub">Important Sensitive File Locations</h3>
      <table class="cmp-table">
        <tbody><tr>
          <th>Data Type</th>
          <th class="win-col">Windows Location</th>
