    </section>

    <!-- 6. AUTHENTICATION -->
    <section class="chapter" id="authentication">
      <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">06 — Authentication</span>Proving Who You Are</h2>
      <p>Authentication is just a fancy word for "How do I know you're you?" Computers use three main ways to check:</p>
      <ul class="body-list">
        <li><strong>Something you know:</strong> A password or a PIN.</li>
        <li><strong>Something you have:</strong> A physical key, a smartphone, or a smart card.</li>
        <li><strong>Something you are:</strong> Your fingerprint or your face (FaceID/Windows Hello).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="sub">Windows: The Ticket System</h3>
      <p>Modern Windows uses a system called **Kerberos**. Imagine you go to a carnival. You pay once at the gate and
        get a roll of tickets. Instead of paying cash at every ride, you just show a ticket. Kerberos works the same
        way—you log in once, and the computer gives you "tickets" to use all your apps without typing your password
        again and again.</p>

      <h3 class="sub">Linux: SSH Keys</h3>
      <p>For remote work, Linux users love **SSH Keys**. Instead of a password, you have two digital files: a "Public
        Key" (the lock) and a "Private Key" (the physical key). You put the lock on your server, and only your private
        key can open it. It's much safer than a password because it's almost impossible to guess.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 7. NETWORK SECURITY -->
    <section class="chapter" id="network">
      <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">07 — Network</span>Internet Safety &amp; Firewalls</h2>
      <p>The internet is like a busy street. A **Firewall** is like a fence around your house that keeps unwanted
        strangers out.</p>

      <h3 class="sub">The Three Levels of Trust (Windows)</h3>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🏠</div>
          <div class="card-title">Home Network</div>
          <p>You trust everyone here. Your computer is visible to others (like a printer or a shared folder).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🏢</div>
          <div class="card-title">Work Network</div>
          <p>Moderate safety. Some things are shared, but the "gate" is a bit more closed.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">☕</div>
          <div class="card-title">Public Network</div>
          <p>Like a coffee shop or airport. The "gate" is locked tight. Your computer hides so others can't find it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub">Linux: The Gatekeeper</h3>
      <p>Linux uses tools like **ufw** (Uncomplicated Firewall) to manage its gates. You can tell it: "Only let my
        friends in through the back door (SSH), and keep the front door locked."</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 8. PROCESS SECURITY -->
    <section class="chapter" id="processes">
      <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">08 — Processes</span>Advanced Safety Tricks</h2>
      <p>Modern computers have some really cool tricks to stop hackers even if they find a way in.</p>

      <h3 class="sub">ASLR: Shuffling the Deck</h3>
      <p>Imagine a hacker knows that the "Secret Safe" is always at 123 Main Street. **ASLR** is a trick where the
        computer changes the address of every "building" every time it starts. One day the safe is at 100 Oak St, the
        next it's at 500 Pine St. The hacker can't find anything!</p>

      <h3 class="sub">DEP: Read-Only Rules</h3>
      <p>Computers have two types of "paper": one you can write on (Data) and one you can read from (Programs). **DEP**
        makes sure that if a hacker writes a "fake program" on the writing paper, the computer refuses to read it. It
        says: "Hey, that's not a real program, that's just some scribbles!"</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Analogy</strong>
        ASLR is like a city where the houses move every night. DEP is like a library where you're allowed to read the
        books, but you're never allowed to follow instructions written in the margins by other readers.
      </div>

