    <!-- 9. MALWARE -->
    <section class="chapter" id="malware">
      <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">09 — Malware</span>Nasty Software (Malware)</h2>
      <p>Malware is short for "Malicious Software." These are programs designed to hurt your computer or steal your
        data.</p>

      <h3 class="sub">Common Types of Malware</h3>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🦠</div>
          <div class="card-title">Viruses</div>
          <p>These hide inside normal programs. When you open the program, the virus wakes up and spreads.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🪱</div>
          <div class="card-title">Worms</div>
          <p>These are like viruses but smarter—they can spread themselves across the internet automatically without you
            doing anything.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🐎</div>
          <div class="card-title">Trojans</div>
          <p>Named after the Trojan Horse, these look like helpful software (like a free game) but hide a nasty surprise
            inside.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">💰</div>
          <div class="card-title">Ransomware</div>
          <p>This locks all your files and demands money (a ransom) to give them back. It's the most dangerous type for
            businesses.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout warn">
        <strong>Myth Buster</strong>
        Some people think "Macs or Linux don't get viruses." That's not true! Any computer can get malware if you're not
        careful.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 10. HARDENING -->
    <section class="chapter" id="hardening">
      <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">10 — Hardening</span>Making Your Computer "Tough"</h2>
      <p>**Hardening** is just a fancy word for making your computer harder to hack. Here's a simple checklist:</p>

      <h3 class="sub">Simple Safety Checklist</h3>
      <ul class="body-list">
        <li>✅ **Update Everything:** Updates fix the "holes" that hackers use to get in.</li>
        <li>✅ **Don't use "Admin" for everything:** Only use the boss account when you really need to.</li>
        <li>✅ **Turn off what you don't use:** If you don't use a feature (like "Remote Desktop"), turn it off. A closed
          door can't be walked through!</li>
        <li>✅ **Strong Passwords:** Use long passwords or a password manager.</li>
        <li>✅ **Backup Your Files:** If you have a copy of your photos on a separate drive, a hacker can't "kidnap" them
          for money.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- 11. LOGGING -->
    <section class="chapter" id="logging">
      <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">11 — Logging</span>The Security Camera (Logging)</h2>
      <p>If someone breaks into your house, you'd want to have a security camera. On a computer, this is called
        **Logging**. It's a diary that records everything that happens.</p>

      <h3 class="sub">What do Logs look like?</h3>
      <ul class="body-list">
        <li><strong>Logon Events:</strong> "User 'Alice' logged in at 10:00 AM."</li>
        <li><strong>Failed Attempts:</strong> "Someone tried to guess the password 100 times in 1 minute." (Red Flag!)
        </li>
        <li><strong>New Apps:</strong> "A new program was installed."</li>
      </ul>
      <p>In Windows, you use the **Event Viewer** to see these. In Linux, they are usually text files kept in a folder
        called `/var/log`.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 12. CASE STUDIES -->
    <section class="chapter" id="vulnerabilities">
      <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">12 — Case Studies</span>Real-World Stories</h2>
      <p>Why do we care about all these settings and rules? Because when they fail, big things happen. Here are a few
        famous stories:</p>

      <h3 class="sub">1. The "WannaCry" Attack (Windows)</h3>
      <p>In 2017, a virus called WannaCry spread around the world in just a few days. It locked computers in hospitals,
        banks, and schools. It used a "hole" in Windows that many people hadn't patched (updated) yet. This is why
        **Updates** are the #1 rule of security!</p>

      <h3 class="sub">2. The "Dirty COW" Bug (Linux)</h3>
      <p>For 9 years, there was a tiny mistake in the Linux "brain" (the Kernel). It allowed a regular user to act like
        the ultimate boss (**root**). Even though Linux is very safe, this story reminds us that even the smartest
        people make mistakes, and we always need to be looking for bugs.</p>

      <h3 class="sub">3. The "Log4Shell" Scare</h3>
      <p>In 2021, a huge bug was found in a tiny piece of software used by almost every big company (like Apple and
        Minecraft). It allowed hackers to take over servers just by sending a simple text message. It showed that
        security isn't just about your computer—it's about every single piece of code you use.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 13. COMPARISON SUMMARY -->
    <section class="chapter" id="comparison">
      <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">13 — Summary</span>Conclusion</h2>
      <p>Whether you use Windows or Linux, the goal is the same: **Keep the bad guys out and your data safe.**</p>
      <ul class="body-list">
        <li><strong>Windows</strong> is great because it has everything built-in and is easy to use for businesses.</li>
        <li><strong>Linux</strong> is great because it's transparent (anyone can check the code) and very powerful for
          servers.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The best security isn't just about having the best software; it's about being smart, keeping things updated,
        and not giving everyone "Admin" powers!</p>
    </section>

